{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Your foes</b> — two times: תהרוס קמיך (you destroy Your foes). שאון קמיך עולה תמיד (the roar of Your foes rises continually). Therefore תהרוס קמיך (you destroy Your foes)." "<b>You send</b> — ה' תשלח חרונך (YHWH, You send Your wrath). מבצריהם תשלח (from their fortresses You send). Teaches: just as they were smitten at the sea, so they were smitten in Egypt, and lifted from their fortresses. And the other: שלח תשלח את האם (you shall surely send the mother). תשלח רוחך יבראון (You send Your Spirit, they are created). But Israel passed through the sea on dry land, as written: תשלח קציריה עד ים (You send the harvest to the sea) — nonetheless they did not enter the land, as written: שלח תשלח את האם ואת הבנים תקח לך (you shall surely send the mother and the young you shall take for yourself) — that He sent the fathers from before Him and caused them to die in the wilderness, and took the young and brought them into the land. Nonetheless they have a portion in the World to Come, as written: תשלח רוחך יבראון (You send Your Spirit, they are created)."], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The argument appears to be: the phrase תהרוס קמיך occurs twice in Scripture, and the second occurrence (Psalms 92:10, 'שאון קמיך עלו תמיד') explains why destruction is warranted — their roar rises continually.", 1 "The verse cited as 'תשלח קציריה עד ים' does not correspond to any standard biblical verse; rendered hyper-literally as it stands. The phrase 'מבצריהם תשלח' also does not match any known verse and is translated as found."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:13:07.483730598Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}